    stageAria: 'Stage {0} of {1}: {2} of {3} words discovered', toDiscover: '{0} more words to discover.', emptyStage: 'Your first baby elephant is born in your first session.',
    ageNames: ['Newborn', 'Calf', 'Juvenile', 'Adult', 'Herd elder'],
    rulesText: [
      ['Five minutes a day', 'Éléfiches brings each word back just before you forget it. Five minutes every day beat one hour once.'],
      ['One word, one elephant', 'A baby elephant is born for each word you discover. It grows with every right answer and gets younger when you forget. It has five ages, one per box.'],
      ['Stages', 'A deck is covered in stages of ten words. The trunk grows as you go, and the tuft of tender grass marks the finish: once it is eaten, you are done.'],
      ['Today’s appetite', 'You choose how many new words you discover per session: three, five or eight. Reviews are added automatically.'],
      ['Exercises that ask more and more', 'First recognise the meaning. Then find the word in your head before seeing the choices, type it in a sentence, rebuild a sentence, and finally type it in a situation. The stronger the memory, the more effort the exercise asks for.'],
      ['How was it?', 'After a right answer, you say whether it was easy, whether you hesitated or whether it was a lucky guess. A word found by luck comes back soon: you are the one who gains.'],
      ['The stage challenge', 'When the ten words of a stage are discovered, a challenge brings them together the next day: match, complete, use in a situation. With one mistake at most, you win a pair of tusks. It moves no elephant.'],
      ['Mistakes', 'A missed word simply comes back sooner. No points are lost and there is no penalty. A written word must be exact: with its accents, even on a capital letter (“École”), and, in French and Spanish, with its article when the question asks for it (“un café”). For one letter, one accent or the article, you get a second try. The keys under your answer give you the accented letters, and the ⇧ key their capitals.'],
      ['Your plan', 'When your teacher has advised a number of days for a list, Éléfiches offers you three paces: Velvet paw, Trotting paw, Hurried paw. Each pace trades minutes a day for days: a few minutes for longer, or the reverse. No pace makes you a better student than another, and you can switch every day. Before choosing, you can tap the words you can already write: you prove them in writing and your plan is calculated on the others. Each day has its programme, to do in one or two sittings, until “day done”. Words missed or answered with help can be asked again the same day, in a second sitting. On the last day, a full recap asks again the words you have not reviewed; “Review everything today” starts it whenever you like, once a day. To change pace, tap one of the three paces on the card; the paw trail opens your plan. The minutes shown are estimates, recalculated every day from your own speed.'],
    ],
